## Check-in answers (5 minutes in)

1. **Compose pointer loop.** One `pointerInput` coroutine per gesture. On down over the *selected* boundary it calls `ScheduleEditor.beginDrag(i, down.uptimeMillis)`, creates a `Channel.CONFLATED` (bounded: latest position only) and launches one `editor.runDrag(channel, SystemClock::uptimeMillis)`. Moves `trySend` a `DragSample`; `runDrag` waits `min(next sample, millisUntilHold)` via `withTimeoutOrNull`, so a still finger fires the hold with no polling and no per-frame coroutine. Pointer event time and `nowMillis` are the same uptime clock. Finger up: close channel, join, `endDrag()`. Cancel/disposal: `finally { channel.close(); abortDrag() }`. Tests drive `runDrag` with `runTest` virtual time and a real channel.
2. **Which schedule opens.** The editor has an explicit target (`Every day` or `One date`). It opens on today's one-date override when one exists, otherwise on repeating. The header names the target; Apply writes exactly that target; switching target is disabled while dirty. Apply on a stale draft (stored target changed since it opened) asks to replace or reload. Repeating Apply states that it also removes today's one-date schedule.
3. **Jitter.** Tolerance is density-aware (3 dp, passed by the UI) and anchored cumulatively at the last timer reset, so slow drift beyond it resets the timer. Focused tests below.
